# key enums and functions reflected across a module (D4a, G32-G41): handlers in a table of function values, a type
# whose key enum another module's generic reads - the importer sees the exported functions, this module all of its own
import me "keyenummod"

# where a handler answers, and the handler
type Route struct(Path String&, Handler fn&(q String&) String) ? {
    if not Path.StartsWith("/"): error ArgumentError.Invalid("Route: Path.StartsWith(\"/\")")
    Path
    Handler
}

# a command-line word, and what it does
type Command struct(Word String&, Run fn&(a I64) I64, Help String& = "") { Word, Run, Help }

fn Users(q String&) String { return "users:" + $q }

fn Items(q String&) String { return "items:" + $q }

fn Add(a I64, b I64 = 10) I64 { return a + b }

fn Plain(x I64) I64 { return x * 2 }

fn private(x I64) I64 { return x - 1 }

# the routes and the commands, a table of function values
Routes := Array<Route>[Route("/users", Users)!, Route("/items", Items)!]
Commands := Array<Command>[Command("add", addOne, "adds two numbers"), Command("hidden", private)]

fn addOne(a I64) I64 { return Add(a) }

# a record with a renamed field: its key enum, Rec.Keys, lists A as "alpha" and leaves B out
type Rec struct(A I64, B I64 = 2) { A, B }

type Rec.Keys enum {
    A = "alpha"
}

fn (r Rec&) Sum() I64 { return r.A + r.B }

fn (r Rec&) Twice(k I64) I64 { return (r.A + r.B) * k }

# this module's own functions, private ones included, through an import of itself
fn OwnNames() String {
    s := ""
    for f in me.Functions {
        if f.Params.Len() == 1: s = $s + $f.Name + " "
    }
    return s
}

test "a module walks its own functions, private ones too, through an import of itself" {
    assert OwnNames() == "Users Items Plain private addOne "
}
